{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqgw7oy7b8z","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"OPERATIONAL BUG: daemon has no singleton guard — repeated spawns pile up and wedge port 8787","summary":"During the codex-parity session the orchestrator daemon wedged (curl /ping → HTTP 000 timeout, MCP calls → ECONNREFUSED), which blocked the CDX-3 judge mid-verdict. Root cause: 3-4 concurrent `node daemon.js` instances were running and fighting over port 8787 (observed PIDs 36392, 60356, 60944, plus a start-daemon.js at 40760); one held the LISTEN socket but never answered requests (wedged), while a fresh spawn logged 'port 8787 busy (predecessor draining?)' 30× then exited. There is no pidfile/port singleton guard on daemon boot: every MCP self-boot / start-daemon hook spawns a daemon when /ping fails, so transient ping failures or concurrent sessions accumulate zombie instances; daemon.js's busy-port retry loop gives up rather than reaping the wedged holder. RECOVERY (worked cleanly): enumerate all `node daemon.js` PIDs (Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8787 + Win32_Process CommandLine), kill ALL of them, confirm :8787 free, start ONE fresh `node daemon.js` (ORCH_CLIENT/ZONOID_HARNESS unset) — it loaded the graph from disk and the overlay state was intact (merge_attempt for the in-flight task succeeded post-restart). FIX DIRECTION: a boot-time singleton guard — if a HEALTHY daemon answers /ping, refuse to spawn; if an unhealthy process owns the port, reap it (or fail loudly) before binding; consider a pidfile + liveness check. Related instability in the same window: note-mqgufbod1fe (judging-gate/overlay desync that forced CDX-3's force:true claim).","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T17:05:16.303Z","vec":[-0.12171931564807892,0.0039032993372529745,0.019156835973262787,0.006498971953988075,0.06014273315668106,-0.0527782216668129,-0.020070452243089676,0.032458122819662094,-0.0037354505620896816,0.07143498212099075,0.013232066296041012,-0.0026760154869407415,0.003798704827204